* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    rc = !ps_script(script)
*
*    script - PowerShell text.  Lines are field marks.
*
*    WHY THIS EXISTS.  Creating a Windows account needs a password, and the
*    obvious "net user <name> <password> /add" puts that password in a process
*    command line, where any local user can read it through Task Manager,
*    Get-CimInstance Win32_Process or ETW.  PROJECT_STATUS.md section 8
*    rejected exactly that pattern for batch login and the same reasoning
*    applies here.  Decision from the repository owner, 14 Aug 2026: write the
*    script to a file instead, run it, delete it.
*
*    The file is written inside the SDSYS directory deliberately.  The
*    installer breaks ACL inheritance on C:\ProgramData\SD and grants
*    narrowly (section 5.7), and anything created underneath inherits that
*    restricted ACL - NTFS applies inheritance in the kernel at creation time,
*    below the MSYS2 runtime, which is why it still works even though the
*    mount is noacl and chmod is a no-op.  So the temporary script is
*    protected by work already done, with no icacls call of its own.
*
*    IT IS WRITTEN TO THE CURRENT DIRECTORY AND NAMED RELATIVELY, and that is
*    not fussiness.  PowerShell is a Windows program and cannot open
*    /usr/local/sdsys/...; BASIC has no way to make a Windows pathname, since
*    OS$FULLPATH returns a POSIX one despite what its comment claims.  A
*    relative name sidesteps the whole problem: the child inherits SD's
*    working directory, which LOGIN set to the account directory, so a bare
*    record name means the same file to both.  It works whether SDSYS is at a
*    POSIX path or a Windows one, which matters while the layout move in
*    section 5.8 is half done.
*
*    Invoke-Expression rather than "& .\script.ps1", for two reasons measured
*    on 14 Aug 2026: the ampersand form does not propagate the script's exit
*    status - a script ending "exit 7" gave 1 - and running a .ps1 file is
*    subject to the execution policy, while running text is not.  So this
*    needs no -ExecutionPolicy Bypass anywhere.
*
*    THE FILE IS DELETED WHETHER THE SCRIPT SUCCEEDED OR NOT.  If SD dies
*    between the write and the delete, a script holding a password is left in
*    the SDSYS directory; it is named $PS.TMP.<user number> so it is
*    recognisable, and the ACL is what is protecting it in the meantime.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function ps_script(script)
$catalog !ps_script

   set.status 0
   rc = -1

   if script = '' then
      set.status 1
      return rc
   end

   id = '$PS.TMP.' : @userno

   openpath '.' to sys.f else
      set.status 1
      return rc
   end

   write script to sys.f, id on error
      set.status 1
      return rc
   end

*  SH1 names PowerShell, so the command IS PowerShell.  The file is named
*  relative to the working directory the child inherits - see the description.
*  Single quotes, so the leading $ of the name is literal to PowerShell.
   q = char(39)

   os.execute 'Get-Content -LiteralPath ' : q : id : q : ' -Raw | Invoke-Expression'
   rc = os.error()

   delete sys.f, id
   close sys.f

return rc
end

* END-CODE
